---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import InterferometerDiagram from '../../components/InterferometerDiagram.astro';
import armTiming from '../../assets/research/arm-length-pulse-timing.jpg';
import countDrift from '../../assets/research/apd-count-drift.jpg';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Cavity phase readout"
  description="Building a fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer to read the reflection phase of a silicon-vacancy nanophotonic cavity — a phase-readout subsystem in the Lukin group at Harvard."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2026 · Lukin group, Harvard</p>
      <h1>A cavity phase interferometer</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        A fibre Mach–Zehnder “phasecheck” interferometer that reads the
        <em>phase</em> of light reflected from a silicon-vacancy nanophotonic
        cavity — the single-node phase-readout subsystem behind the group's
        SiV-based entanglement program.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Visiting Undergraduate Research Fellow</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">fibre interferometry</span>
            <span class="tag">heterodyne detection</span>
            <span class="tag">phase locking</span>
            <span class="tag">cavity QED</span>
            <span class="tag">instrument control</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        For most of 2026 I built and characterised a fibre interferometer whose
        one job is to measure the phase of light coming back from an on-chip
        silicon-vacancy (SiV) cavity. It is a small piece of a much larger
        machine — the Lukin group's effort to entangle separated SiV centres
        across nodes — but it is a piece the rest of the program leans on, and
        it was mine to make work. What follows is an honest account of a
        prototype: balanced arms and a locking loop that run, not yet a fully
        characterised instrument.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="why phase" />

      <h2>Why phase, not intensity</h2>
      <p>
        A recurring problem in this kind of cavity QED is telling an
        over-coupled cavity apart from an under-coupled one. In a plain
        reflection spectrum they can look almost identical: the reflected
        <em>intensity</em> dips in both cases. What separates them is the phase
        of the reflection coefficient across the optical transition — one state
        sweeps through a full 2π, the other barely moves. Phase is the clean
        discriminator, and a bare photodiode throws it away. So the instrument
        interferes the cavity's reflection against a stable reference arm and
        reads the relative phase out of the fringe.
      </p>

      <h2>A two-laser heterodyne design</h2>
      <p>
        The setup is a fibre Mach–Zehnder interferometer. Light is split into a
        signal arm that routes through a circulator to the cavity on the fridge
        and back, and a reference arm built around a roughly 46 m fibre spool.
        Two lasers share the path: a narrow-linewidth reference laser that is
        stable but fixed near the 737 nm SiV line, and a tunable probe laser
        that is broad and drifty but can sweep several GHz across the cavity
        resonance. The stable laser defines the phase reference; the tunable one
        interrogates the cavity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trick that makes the phase readable is a pair of acousto-optic
        modulators (AOMs), one in each arm, driven at slightly offset RF
        frequencies. Their difference frequency puts a heterodyne beat note on
        the combined light, so the cavity phase rides on the phase of an
        oscillating APD signal rather than sitting in a DC level. If both arms
        were at the same optical frequency you would be doing homodyne
        detection — a static voltage buried in intensity noise and electronic
        drift. Shifting one arm by a controllable RF offset lifts the phase up
        onto a beat note that a digitiser can fit cleanly.
      </p>
      <figure class="fig diagram">
        <InterferometerDiagram />
        <figcaption>
          Schematic of the setup, after my own drawing. Combined probe and
          phase-locking light splits into a signal arm — in-fibre AOM, 99:1 tap,
          then a circulator routing it up to the SiV nanophotonic cavity — and a
          reference arm through an adjustable attenuator and a ~46 m fibre spool.
          The arms recombine on a 50:50 coupler read out on two APDs, whose
          signals drive the servo (dashed) that phase-locks the two in-fibre
          AOMs. The lower panel shows the probe (Toptica) and lock (TiSa) lasers
          being gated, combined, and sent to the interferometer input.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="the build" />

      <h2>From a setup under a table to fringes</h2>
      <p>
        The project started as salvage. An earlier phase-lock interferometer had
        been dismantled and pushed under a bench; recovering it meant literally
        crawling underneath to trace what was left. Rather than resurrect the
        whole thing, I rebuilt from the simplest possible version — a bare
        Mach–Zehnder with two beam splitters and a photodiode — and grew it from
        there.
      </p>
      <p>
        A lot of the early time went into unglamorous fibre work. Free-space to
        single-mode coupling was punishing: 16 mW in free space could collapse to
        a quarter of a milliwatt after the fibre, and on bad days to nanowatts,
        and a nudged collimator could lose the coupling entirely. I learned to
        splice fibre by hand, characterised the in-fibre polarisers (well over
        50% insertion loss, which forced a careful polarisation budget), and
        found that a two-beam-splitter MZI drifted far less than the long-fibre
        version — a blunt confirmation that the fibre length and polarisation
        drift, not the lasers, were the dominant noise. The first stable
        sinusoidal fringes were a genuinely good day. Adding the two AOMs in
        burst mode from an arbitrary-waveform generator turned that into a
        working heterodyne interferometer, though the interference still drifted
        on a ~1 Hz timescale.
      </p>

      <h2>Balancing the arms</h2>
      <p>
        A Mach–Zehnder only gives a clean, frequency-flat fringe if the two arms
        are close to path-matched; a length mismatch imprints a steep geometric
        phase ramp as you sweep the laser. My first attempt to measure the
        imbalance — pulsing the AOMs and watching arrival times on a scope — was
        too coarse: resolving a 1 cm path difference needs about 300 ps of timing
        resolution, well past what that method gave. Switching to a burst-timing
        measurement through the AOMs and the APD signal chain finally resolved it.
      </p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={armTiming} alt="Two normalized-count pulse curves versus time-since-sync, one per arm, offset by about 9.5 nanoseconds, each with an error-function box fit." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Per-arm pulse-arrival measurement: the reference-AOM and lock-AOM
          bursts land ~9.5 ns apart, corresponding to about 1.95 m of path
          difference per measurement. The aggregate arm imbalance came out near
          12 m, with cm-scale run-to-run variation I never fully explained.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The aggregate imbalance landed around 12 m — larger than I expected, and
        oddly variable at the centimetre level between runs. I compensated it the
        direct way: adding roughly 12 m of matching fibre to the free arm so that,
        with the motorised delay stage parked near 120 mm, the two arms come out
        balanced and the delay stage has headroom to trim on either side.
      </p>

      <h2>Locking the phase</h2>
      <p>
        Even with balanced arms, thermal and acoustic drift in the fibre walks
        the phase around, so the interferometer needs active locking. A naive
        scheme that just stepped through phase indices did not hold. What worked
        better was calibrating an error signal first: sweep the AOM-inserted
        phase across [0, 2π], measure the difference of the two APD channels,
        and read off the slope at the operating point to set the gain of a
        proportional-integral controller. The lock partially holds — it reduces
        the drift but does not yet kill it — and getting the acquisition fast
        enough (tens of milliseconds per cycle) was a real constraint on the loop.
      </p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={countDrift} alt="Two APD count-rate traces over 30 seconds oscillating in anti-phase between roughly 400 and 900 thousand counts per second." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Residual drift after length compensation: the two APD output ports
          swing in anti-phase over 30 s — the signature of slow phase drift
          moving power between the interferometer's two outputs. Suppressing this
          is exactly what the lock is meant to do, and where the work stands now.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The control side grew alongside the optics. The Newport DL235 delay stage
        runs from Python drivers I wrote (after untangling its status and error
        codes so it actually waits for motion to finish); the oscilloscope,
        arbitrary-waveform generator, time tagger and DC supply are all networked
        through pylabnet, and I pulled them together into a single GUI so the
        whole stack drives from one interface. Late in the project we brought in a
        RedPitaya to orchestrate the lock — SSH-accessible with its oscilloscope
        feature working, though its vector-analyser mode I never got running.
      </p>

      <CriticalityDivider label="status" />

      <h2>What I picked up</h2>
      <p>
        Concretely, the build taught me fibre splicing and handling; polarisation
        control and polarimetry across a multi-component fibre setup; RF
        electronics for AOM drive (amplifier gain, burst-mode sequencing, drive
        limits); laser frequency behaviour and wavemeter-feedback tuning; and a
        fair amount of instrument-control software — device drivers, networked
        client/server control, and a GUI to sit on top of it all.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        As of July 2026 the interferometer is physically built, the arms are
        balanced, and a PI locking loop runs — but it is a working prototype, not
        a finished instrument. The lock is only partially stable, the residual
        drift above is not yet suppressed to where it needs to be, and full
        characterisation is ongoing. It is the honest state of an apprentice
        build: the hard parts mostly work, and the last, fussiest part —
        holding the phase rock-steady — is still in progress.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .shell { max-width: 44rem; }
  .back { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .back a, .proj-foot a { color: var(--muted); }
  .back a:hover, .proj-foot a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  .proj-head { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .proj-head h1 { margin: 0.15em 0 0.3em; font-size: var(--step-3); }
  .proj-blurb {
    font-size: 1.24rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 38rem;
  }
  .proj-meta {
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .proj-meta > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .proj-meta dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .proj-meta dd { margin: 0; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem 0.5rem; }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--accent-deep);
    background: var(--accent-wash);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  .fig { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--faint);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: #fff;
  }
  .fig.diagram { margin: 2.2rem 0; }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .proj-foot {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--faint);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .proj-meta > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
